sensor-control (1.4.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * sr all, sr internal and sr external name on standard error each driver
    that exits with an error, prints nothing, or prints something other than
    an array of readings, and leave it out. A failed driver used to vanish
    silently, and when every driver failed sr printed [] and exited 0, which
    read as a node without sensors; it now prints nothing and exits 1. A
    driver that prints readings but exits with an error is named, and its
    readings are kept
  * sr runs the drivers when it cannot write to /var/log/sensor-control, as
    when it is run by a user other than root. bash does not run a command
    whose redirection fails, so no driver ran: sr all printed [], sr check
    reported every sensor missing and sr <device> blamed a driver that never
    started. The drivers' messages go to sr's standard error instead, and
    LOG_DIR sets another directory
  * An interrupt or SIGTERM ends sr at once, with status 130 or 143, and stops
    the drivers still running. The signals were caught without exiting: sr all
    carried on, deleted the output of the drivers that had finished, printed
    what was left and exited 0, and the drivers ran on to their own timeout
  * sr <device> list, setup, enable, version and identify run the driver's own
    command, with its output and exit status passed on and no timeout. Only
    list was passed on, so "sudo sr bme680 enable" changed config.txt but was
    reported as invalid JSON, and its "REBOOT REQUIRED" was discarded
  * sr checks that a driver prints one array of readings, not merely valid
    JSON. A bare object gave a blank line and exit 0 from sr <device>, and
    null from sr all
  * sr's message for a driver that exits 20 no longer blames the argument
    alone, since a driver also exits 20 when it cannot produce readings, and
    names the log that holds the driver's own message. sr <device> exits 124
    for a driver the timeout stopped, and 137 for one it had to kill, rather
    than 143 or whatever status the driver gave on SIGTERM
  * A reading without a timestamp is given the time at which its driver wrote
    its output, not the time sr started, which for a driver started late or
    slow to read was seconds earlier
  * sr internal --mock and sr external --mock keep readings by their internal
    field; both returned every mock reading. --mock on its own reads every
    device, as a filter on its own does, rather than printing usage
  * sr list, sr <device> list and --mock no longer need a serial from pi-data.
    Under --mock a node without one has node_id "mock", with a warning, and a
    read that needs the serial says where it comes from
  * sr check run by a relative path, as ./sr or bash sr, checks with its own
    sr all rather than the first sr on PATH, and works without the executable
    bit
  * sr list discards what a driver prints for identify and gives it no input,
    so a sensor-* program that is not a driver can neither add names to the
    list nor swallow the rest of it, and skips anything that is not a file
  * sr's usage text exists once and shows the device argument. Its version
    comes from dpkg, then from the changelog
  * sensor-onboard checks every figure before using it. A missing thermal zone
    gave onboard_cpu a valid 0.000 C, free without a "Mem:" row, as in a
    German locale, dropped memory_used, and an empty storage figure lost all
    four readings. A figure that cannot be read is now a failed reading, with
    value null and the reason in error, and free runs in the C locale
  * sensor-onboard keeps the sign of a GPU temperature below 0 C, which was
    published as positive, and treats vcgencmd output without exactly one
    number as a failed read
  * sensor-onboard's usage line lists -v and version. Its version comes from
    the sr installed beside it, not the first sr on PATH, and is "unknown"
    rather than empty when that sr cannot say
  * Packaging: logrotate no longer gives copytruncate with create, which has
    no effect alongside it; sr opens each log afresh on every run. postinst
    sets -e before anything else, sets the owner and mode of /etc/ws only when
    it creates the directory, and loses case labels it is never called with.
    debian/source/options is removed: --exclude is not a dpkg-source option,
    and dpkg-source leaves out .git by default
  * Documentation: the README's return codes swapped 20 and 21. sr(1) and the
    README describe 20 as a rejected argument or no readings, list 124, 137
    and the statuses passed on from a driver, and describe the logs, LOG_DIR,
    failed drivers and a location as a GeoJSON Feature. sensor-onboard(1)
    describes each failed reading. The bash completion offers mock, setup,
    enable and version

 -- Ed Baker <ed@ebaker.me.uk>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:37:27 +0100

sensor-control (1.4.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Documentation: sr(1) and the README describe every field of a reading;
    both omitted device, sensor_name, location and error, and the README also
    omitted deployment_id. sc-prototype(1) listed ten of the fourteen fields.
    The README's guidance for a new driver names libwildlifesystems and
    ws-emit rather than a jq pattern sensor-onboard no longer uses, and the
    CONCURRENCY default is stated correctly
  * Packaging: depend on procps, whose "free" sensor-onboard reads memory
    with, and recommend raspi-utils, whose vcgencmd reads the GPU temperature;
    neither was declared. A postrm removes /var/log/sensor-control on purge,
    and /etc/ws if nothing else has put anything in it; postinst created both
    and nothing removed them. The source stanza loses a Description field it
    should never have had, and the package description says what is in it.
    The lintian override for our "stable" distribution, as libwildlifesystems
    already had
  * The README no longer describes sc-inst, a tool that does not exist;
    devices are installed as packages
  * sr passes a second argument through to the device's driver, so
    "sr onboard onboard_cpu" reads one sensor and "sr dht11 list" lists a
    driver's measurements, as sr(1) and the README have always said. The
    argument was silently discarded and "all" run in its place
  * sr sends its "unknown device" and "unknown sensor" diagnostics to stderr
    like every other, and names what happened: a driver exiting 20 has rejected
    its argument, and 21 is sr's own code for a driver that does not exist. A
    caller piping into jq no longer gets prose on stdout
  * sensor-onboard gains mock, setup and enable, which every C driver already
    had. Without mock, "sr all --mock" silently omitted the onboard sensors and
    "sr check --mock" reported them missing
  * sensor-onboard takes its sensor names from one array for list, usage and
    argument matching, so they cannot drift; the usage line had already lost
    -v and version. Its --version reports this package's version rather than
    ws-emit's, and it checks "pi-data serial" as sr does, emitting sensor_id
    null rather than "_onboard_cpu" when there is no serial
  * sensor-onboard no longer tags storage_used and memory_used with device
    raspberry_pi, which the 1.3.0 entry below said they must not carry but
    ws-emit had no way to express. Requires wildlifesystems-tools 1.3.0, now a
    versioned dependency, for the per-reading --device
  * sr honours SENS_DIR from the environment, so the integration tests can run
    it against freshly built drivers; it was readonly, and the tests silently
    probed /usr/bin. A driver's stderr goes to the same log file whether it
    was run by "sr all" or by name; the two paths used different names
  * sr's version lookup and node-field injection each existed twice; each is
    now one function
  * sr(1): repair the roff that hid --with-errors and the ENVIRONMENT section,
    document --mock, SENS_DIR and NODE_JSON_FILE, and correct the CONCURRENCY
    default. sensor-onboard(1) gains the new commands and an EXIT STATUS. The
    sr completion stops offering --sensor values that could never match and
    completes devices from "sr list" instead
  * Document the sensor script timeout as the 10 seconds sr actually enforces,
    with SIGKILL 5 seconds after SIGTERM. sr(1) and the README both said 5
    seconds
  * sensor-onboard: report the CPU temperature to three decimal places. The
    kernel gives millidegrees and the reading was integer-divided by 1000, so
    47.823 C was reported as 47: two digits of the reading were discarded on the
    way out. Uses awk rather than shell arithmetic, which truncates towards zero
    and would render a sub-zero reading as "-5.-123"
  * sensor-onboard: take the node serial from "pi-data serial", the same source
    sr uses for node_id, instead of parsing /proc/cpuinfo itself. Its
    "cut -d ' ' -f 2" only worked on the exact tab spacing a Raspberry Pi
    happens to emit: given "Serial : X" it returned ":", and given extra spaces
    it returned nothing, so every onboard sensor_id would have been malformed
    while node_id stayed correct
  * sensor-onboard: report a failed GPU temperature read instead of omitting the
    reading. A node where vcgencmd is missing or unreadable silently produced
    three readings rather than four, leaving a consumer unable to tell "this
    node has no GPU sensor" from "this node has stopped reporting one". It now
    reports value null with the reason in error, as the C drivers do for a
    failed read. Note "sensor-onboard onboard_gpu" consequently exits 0 with a
    reported error where it previously exited 20 with no JSON at all
  * sr: copy deployment_id from /etc/ws/node.json into every reading, alongside
    node_id and timestamp. The field has been in the sc-prototype template all
    along and null in every reading ever produced
  * The file is shipped by ws-node and only read here, the same arrangement as
    /etc/ws/expected.sensors. It is optional, and a malformed or unreadable file
    leaves deployment_id null rather than failing the read - losing a metadata
    field is preferable to losing the readings
  * NODE_JSON_FILE overrides the path, mirroring EXPECTED_SENSORS_FILE
  * Document node.json in sr(1) and the README

 -- Ed Baker <ed@ebaker.me.uk>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 17:00:00 +0100

sensor-control (1.3.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * sensor-onboard now builds its readings with ws-emit rather than jq, so it
    emits exactly what the C drivers emit, from the same library code. It gains
    "{{node}}" resolution: onboard readings carried the unresolved token while
    every C driver had begun reporting the node's coordinates
  * sensor-onboard: with no argument it failed with "unbound variable" under
    set -u instead of defaulting to all, as the C drivers do
  * sensor-onboard: --version, -v and version now report a version rather than
    being treated as an unknown sensor name
  * sensor-onboard: an unknown argument exits 20, matching WS_EXIT_INVALID_ARG
    and the C drivers, rather than 21. It also no longer prints a stray "[" and
    an error message into what should be JSON
  * sensor-onboard: a missing or failing jq can no longer produce "[,,,]" with a
    success exit status, jq no longer being involved
  * Depend on wildlifesystems-tools for ws-emit. Drop the bc dependency: the one
    use of it, a percentage, is shell arithmetic now
  * sc-prototype: add "device" and "location" fields. "device" carries the
    physical device model, which "sensor" does not reliably express; "location"
    carries where the sensor sits, as "{{node}}", "{{none}}" or a GeoJSON Point
  * sensor-onboard: report device "raspberry_pi" and location "{{node}}" for
    the SoC temperature sensors; storage_used and memory_used report location
    "{{none}}" and no device, having neither

 -- Ed Baker <ed@ebaker.me.uk>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0100

